3043 (20.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">579 (21.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.025</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(21.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(21.6)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Weekend admission, n (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4090 (27.5)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">761 (28.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.016</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(28.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(28.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">tPA, n (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5261 (35.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">937 (34.7)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.012</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(35.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(35.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Year, n (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;2020</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3752 (25.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">679 (25.2)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.000</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(25.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(25.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;2021</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5176 (34.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">874 (32.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.050</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(32.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(32.8)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;2022</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5964 (40.0)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1144 (42.4)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.048</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(42.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">(42.1)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x0003c;0.001</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>BMI, body mass index; CA, contact aspiration; JCS, Japan Coma Scale; mRS, modified Rankin Scale; SMD, standardized mean difference; SR, stent retriever; tPA, tissue plasminogen activator</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>After propensity score overlap weighting, the baseline characteristics were well balanced between the CA + SR group and the CA group, with a standard mean difference &#x0003c;0.1 in all variables (<bold><xref rid="table-1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref></bold>). The frequency of ICH did not differ significantly between the CA + SR group and the CA group (1.4% vs. 1.3%; aRD, 0.1%; 95% confidence interval [CI], &#x02212;0.5 to 0.5; <bold><xref rid="table-2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref></bold>). The frequency of functional independence at discharge was lower in the CA + SR group than in the CA group (28.7% vs. 36.2%; aRD, &#x02212;7.5%; 95% CI, &#x02212;9.5% to &#x02212;5.4%). The rate of in-hospital mortality was higher in the CA + SR group than in the CA group (11.4% vs. 9.2%; aRD, 2.2%; 95% CI, 1.0% to 3.5%). The total hospitalization cost was significantly higher in the CA + SR group (USD 21687) than in the CA group (USD 18317), with a cost difference of USD 3370 (95% CI: 2976&#x02013;3764).</p><table-wrap position="float" id="table-2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><title>Comparison of outcomes between the CA group and the CA + SR group in the propensity score overlap-weighted cohort</title></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CA + SR group (%)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CA group (%)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">aRD (%)</th><th align="center" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">95% CI (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ICH</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.4</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.3</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.5 to 0.5</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Functional independence at discharge</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.7</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">36.2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;7.5</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;9.5 to &#x02212;5.4</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">In-hospital mortality</td><td align="center" valign="top" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.4</td><td align="center" valign="top" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9.2</td><td align="center" valign="top" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.2</td><td align="center" valign="top" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.0 to 3.5</td></tr><tr><th align="left" valign="middle" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CA + SR group</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CA group</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Difference</th><th align="center" valign="middle" style="border-bottom: thin solid;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">95% CI</th></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Total cost during admission (SD)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">USD 21687 (79)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">USD 18317 (184)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">USD 3370</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">USD 2976 to 3764</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>aRD, adjusted risk difference; CA, contact aspiration; CI, confidence interval; ICH, intracranial hemorrhage; SR, stent retriever; SD, standard deviation</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>The sensitivity analyses were restricted to patients in a comatose state at admission (n = 2631) (<bold><xref rid="s01" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables 1</xref></bold> and <bold><xref rid="s02" ref-type="supplementary-material">2</xref></bold>) and those with atrial fibrillation (n = 10446) (<bold><xref rid="s03" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables 3</xref></bold> and <bold><xref rid="s04" ref-type="supplementary-material">4</xref></bold>). In comatose patients, the frequency of ICH did not differ significantly between the 2 groups (1.0% vs. 1.0%; aRD, &#x02212;0.1%; 95% CI, &#x02212;1.1% to 1.0%). The frequency of functional independence at discharge was lower (11.3% vs. 16.9%; aRD, &#x02212;5.6%; 95% CI, &#x02212;9.5% to &#x02212;1.7%), while the rate of in-hospital mortality was higher (22.5% vs. 16.9%; aRD, 5.6%; 95% CI, 1.6% to 9.7%) in the CA + SR group than in the CA group. In the atrial fibrillation cohort, the frequency of ICH did not differ significantly between the groups (1.5% vs. 1.0%; aRD, 0.5%; 95% CI, &#x02212;0.1% to 1.1%). In the coma cohort, the frequency of functional independence at discharge was lower in the CA + SR group than in the CA group (28.0% vs. 34.6%; aRD, &#x02212;6.6%; 95% CI, &#x02212;9.4% to &#x02212;3.9%). The in-hospital mortality rate was higher in the CA + SR group (10.3% vs. 8.2%; aRD, 2.0%; 95% CI, 0.4% to 3.6%). IV analysis yielded an F-statistic of 578.7. The CA + SR group was not associated with a higher frequency of ICH (odds ratio [OR], 0.78; 95% CI, 0.52&#x02013;1.17), but was associated with a lower frequency of functional independence at discharge (OR, 0.72; 95% CI, 0.64&#x02013;0.81) and higher in-hospital mortality (OR, 1.22; 95% CI, 1.02&#x02013;1.45) (<bold><xref rid="s05" ref-type="supplementary-material">Supplementary Tables 5</xref></bold> and <bold><xref rid="s06" ref-type="supplementary-material">6</xref></bold>).</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="s5"><title>Discussion</title><p>In this study, we compared the outcomes of CA monotherapy with those of CA + SR in patients who underwent mechanical thrombectomy for acute ischemic stroke. The frequency of ICH did not differ significantly between the CA group and the CA + SR group. The CA group exhibited a higher proportion of functional independence at discharge, lower in-hospital mortality, and lower total hospitalization costs.</p><p>Previous trials, such as ASTER2, reported a lower frequency of ICH in the CA + SR group than in the SR-alone group.<sup><xref rid="ref-21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>)</sup> In contrast, our study found that CA monotherapy elicited better functional outcomes than CA + SR, despite similar proportions of ICH. This may reflect recent improvements in CA devices, especially large-bore catheters, which enable efficient recanalization, with lower procedural complexity.<sup><xref rid="ref-7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>,<xref rid="ref-21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="ref-23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>)</sup> CA + SR has been widely used in Japan, where its frequency of application is higher than that in Western countries and China.<sup><xref rid="ref-15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>,<xref rid="ref-24" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>)</sup> This discrepancy may be attributed to differences in patient selection and treatment strategies. Specifically, in real-world practice, patients undergoing CA + SR may represent a high-risk group characterized by a more challenging clot morphology or proximal thrombosis. Despite potential concerns about distal vessel fragility, CA monotherapy was not associated with a higher risk of hemorrhage than CA + SR, probably because the former is less invasive than SR. A smaller vessel diameter and increased tortuosity can lead to excessive mechanical stress and are related to increased hemorrhagic complications during instrumentation in more distal vessels. In addition, a previous study showed that CA alone avoided the deployment and retrieval of a stent catheter, which can cause endothelial damage and increase the risk of distal embolization.<sup><xref rid="ref-5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>)</sup> The superior functional outcomes and lack of difference in the frequency of ICH observed in the CA group may be attributed to the less invasive nature of aspiration.</p><p>The reason for the higher hospitalization costs in CA + SR may be the high cost of an SR, which is approximately USD 2570 per device. Given the high incidence of acute ischemic stroke and the increasing number of thrombectomy procedures performed annually, these cost savings could have substantial implications for healthcare systems.</p><p>Some limitations of this study should be acknowledged. First, we could not clearly define the first-line thrombectomy device used for each case. Specifically, we could not distinguish whether patients initially underwent thrombectomy with a single device and then switched to CA + SR due to procedural difficulty or whether the combined approach was used from the outset. Patients requiring treatment escalation may represent a high-risk population with challenging thrombus characteristics. Therefore, our findings may have underestimated the true benefits of CA + SR. However, the overwhelming predominance of combined cases in our dataset suggests that CA + SR was frequently selected as the initial strategy rather than employed as a rescue strategy after failure of the initial technique. Furthermore, because the results of the IV analysis were similar to the main results, the unmeasured confounders may not have overturned the results. Second, a substantial number of patients who were not treated with CA were excluded from the analysis. This may limit the generalizability of our findings, while selection bias may have influenced the observed outcomes. Third, because the database used in this study did not include imaging information, critical variables such as thrombus location and infarct volume could not be assessed. The absence of imaging data may have introduced unmeasured confounders, although we attempted to address this limitation using alternative clinical indicators, such as consciousness at admission, to estimate stroke severity. Finally, the generalizability to healthcare systems with different treatment protocols or reimbursement structures may be limited because our study was conducted in Japan. Nonetheless, the fundamental principles of mechanical thrombectomy remain consistent globally, suggesting that our findings may be useful in a broader clinical context.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="s6"><title>Conclusion</title><p>There was no significant difference in the incidence of ICH between the CA and CA + SR groups; however, the CA group was associated with better functional outcomes, lower in-hospital mortality, and lower costs. Further prospective studies are required to confirm the results of the present study.</p></sec><sec id="s7" sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Information</title><supplementary-material id="s01" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 1</label><caption><title>Demographic characteristics of comatose patients in the propensity score overlap-weighted cohort</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s02" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 2</label><caption><title>Comparison of outcomes between the CA group and the CA + SR group in the propensity score overlap-weighted cohort of comatose patients</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s03" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 3</label><caption><title>Demographic characteristics of patients with atrial fibrillation in the propensity score overlap-weighted cohort</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s04" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 4</label><caption><title>Comparison of outcomes between the CA group and the CA + SR group in the propensity score overlap-weighted cohort of patients with atrial fibrillation</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s05" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 5</label><caption><title>Demographic characteristics of patients by instrumental variable analysis using the proportion of CA + SR at each hospital</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material><supplementary-material id="s06" position="float" content-type="local-data"><label>Supplementary table 6</label><caption><title>Comparison of outcomes between the CA group and the CA + SR group by instrumental variable analysis using the proportion of combined techniques performed at each hospital</title></caption><media xlink:href="jnet-19-01-2025-0105-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="s9"><title>Disclosure Statement</title><p>The authors declare no conflicts of interest regarding the publication of this article.</p></sec><ref-list id="ref1"><title>References</title><ref id="ref-1"><label>1)</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><string-name><surname>Bracard</surname>
